nis, basketball, handball, badminton, volleyball, squash and some other games", "tags": [], "examples": ["The local sports club has six tennis courts and two squash courts.", "The shuttlecock landed outside the court."], "quotes": [{"text": "By one o'clock the place was choc-a-bloc. […] The restaurant was packed, and the promenade between the two main courts and the subsidiary courts was thronged with healthy-looking youngish people, drawn to the Mecca of tennis from all parts of the country.", "reference": "1935, George Goodchild, chapter 5, in Death on the Centre Court:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": [{"definition": "one of the two divisions of a tennis, badminton or volleyball court, in which the player or players of each team play", "tags": [], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "The photograph at left captures a great serve by Dr. Sadowsky, who will never forget one of Bobby Riggs's serves, which had such a great spin that it landed in his court and bounced back to the other side of the net before he had a chance to return it.", "reference": "2010, Cara Marcus, Faulkner Hospital:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}]}, {"definition": "(ornithology) A space prepared and decorated by certain bird species in which to advertise themselves for a mate.", "tags": [], "examples": ["The male Wilson's bird of paradise clears an area of rainforest to create a court in which to perform an elaborate mating dance."], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": []}, {"language": {"code": "en", "name": "English"}, "partOfSpeech": "verb", "pronunciations": [{"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈkɔːt/", "tags": ["Received Pronunciation"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "[ˈkʰɔːt]", "tags": ["Received Pronunciation"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈkɔɹt/", "tags": ["Canada", "General American"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "[ˈkʰɔɹt]", "tags": ["Canada", "General American"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈko(ː)ɹt/", "tags": []}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈkoət/", "tags": []}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈkoːt/", "tags": ["General Australian", "New Zealand"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "[ˈkʰoːt]", "tags": ["General Australian", "New Zealand"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈko(r)ʈ/", "tags": []}], "forms": [{"word": "courts", "tags": ["present", "singular", "third-person"]}, {"word": "courting", "tags": ["participle", "present"]}, {"word": "courted", "tags": ["participle", "past"]}, {"word": "courted", "tags": ["past"]}], "senses": [{"definition": "(transitive) To seek to achieve or win (a prize).", "tags": ["transitive"], "examples": ["He was courting big new accounts that previous salesmen had not attempted."], "quotes": [{"text": "On the contrary, they employed the brief respite that was left them in fortifying one another's courage, and in bearing testimony to the truth in so earnest a manner that they might almost seem to have courted the crown of martyrdom.", "reference": "1800, William H[ickling] Prescott, History of The Reign of Philip The Second, King of Spain, volume 3:"}, {"text": "Guilt and misery shrink, by a natural instinct, from public notice: they court privacy and solitude: and even in their choice of a grave will sometimes sequester themselves from the general population of the churchyard […]", "reference": "1821, Thomas De Quincey, “To the Reader”, in Confessions of an English Opium-Eater:"}], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(transitive) To risk (a consequence, usually negative).", "tags": ["transitive"], "examples": ["She courted controversy with her frank speeches."], "quotes": [{"text": "It is not unknown for hot axleboxes to fail completely and for wagons to become derailed as a result. Surely it is courting disaster to allow a train to proceed for up to seven miles with a defective vehicle before it can be brought to a halt?", "reference": "1964 April, “Aut